Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["\<\
On Distributions. How A Fnc Identity Opens Up To Different Distribution \
Families.\
\>", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.999241033295565*^9, 3.999241042700261*^9}, {
   3.9992411439745116`*^9, 3.9992411564625006`*^9}, 3.999346239382189*^9, 
   3.9993494305777626`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"dd233668-44d9-4c89-a2f2-\
dcdd3b64b548"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["[see -> BSSRDF -> Correlated Media -> Casasanta & Garra 2018 vs \
Jarabo/Wrenninge Gamma-mixture]\n\n",
  FontSize->12,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.5]],
 "Two transmittance laws, from two different fields, turn out to be exactly \
the same formula at a specific point (\[Alpha]=1). Despite this, the \
underlying constructions are completely different: one starts from a fixed \
event rate and a reweighted event count, while the other introduces a \
Gamma-distributed random rate.\n\nThis notebook investigates the probability \
model connecting the two. We derive the role of \[Alpha], examine the \
\[Alpha]\[RightArrow]\[Infinity] limit and its relation to classical \
transport, and eventually identify the resulting distribution as the Lomax \
distribution. Along the way, we compare the probabilistic construction with \
the formulations given in the relevant literature. \n\nAll just as an excuse \
to dive into distribution mechanics for light transport theory. \n"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.999240116176013*^9, 3.999240117487582*^9}, {
   3.9993491385473375`*^9, 3.9993493439010468`*^9}, {3.9993497984184914`*^9, 
   3.999349898281293*^9}, {3.999349964639141*^9, 3.9993499648065677`*^9}, {
   3.9993557273128967`*^9, 3.9993557445877056`*^9}, {3.999357503633033*^9, 
   3.999357533013809*^9}, 3.9993576097559094`*^9, {3.9993576477620964`*^9, 
   3.9993576675977383`*^9}, {3.999357699475585*^9, 
   3.999357704148697*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a13047aa-05e4-4ae4-880d-\
d93ef2d16fce"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Two Source Formulas", "Subchapter",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9993482992211714`*^9, 
  3.9993482997728395`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4e209da3-6703-4560-9938-\
67e36cee8172"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["[see -> Casasanta & Garra 2018 - Towards a Generalized \
Beer-Lambert Law, Eq. 6, Section 4]\n",
  FontSize->12,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.5]],
 "Their inhomogeneous weighted-Poisson construction, at the Mittag-Leffler \
weight's \[Nu]=1 special case, collapses to a plain hyperbolic law. As stated \
in their own Eq. 6, using x0 for their constant:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9993559821211524`*^9, 
  3.999355983101917*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6a4d713f-fba4-4089-829d-\
874360bf2c0a"],

Cell[BoxData[
 StyleBox[
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"T", "(", "x", ")"}], "=", 
   FractionBox["1", 
    RowBox[{"x", "+", "x0"}]]}],
  FontSize->24,
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0.5, 0]]], "DisplayFormula",ExpressionUUID->\
"01cdb982-981d-4a1c-a649-1ea438b70741"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Note this does ",
 StyleBox["not",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " satisfy T(0)=1, which any genuine survival function must (a photon must \
survive zero distance with certainty). Their own stated equation is not \
properly normalized for use as a Monte Carlo free-flight distribution. The \
corrected form, with x0 now playing the role of a genuine scale parameter, is:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8b55ce54-17a8-4b31-9f28-54408ad6fcc7"],

Cell[BoxData[
 StyleBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["T", "hyp"], "(", "x", ")", "=", 
   FractionBox["x0", 
    RowBox[{"x", "+", "x0"}]]}],
  FontSize->24,
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0.5, 0]]], "DisplayFormula",ExpressionUUID->\
"c6b77913-3e8d-4603-9446-3dc5036707ec"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["x0",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " is the only free parameter here, and it is not an arbitrary curve-fitting \
constant -- it is the scale at which the medium\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \
(deterministic, in this construction) density profile \[Lambda](x)=ln(x+x0) \
actually varies. This formula comes from a weighted-Poisson mechanism: the \
extinction ",
 StyleBox["rate itself stays fixed and deterministic",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " at every point -- what gets reweighted is the ",
 StyleBox["probability of the event count",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", not the rate. There is no randomization of \[Sigma] anywhere in this \
derivation, which is exactly why it sits outside the \
Jensen\[CloseCurlyQuote]s-inequality bound that constrains the next formula \
below."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.999355854312027*^9, 
  3.9993558545599785`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"10657582-72e1-4c21-9a76-\
269810f8cf32"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["[see -> \nJarabo, Aliaga & Gutierrez 2018 (5.2 - An intuitive \
local model for positively-correlated media); \nWrenninge, Villemin & Hery \
2017, Pixar Technical Memo 17-07]\n\n",
  FontSize->12,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.5]],
 "The Gamma-mixture transmittance, general \[Alpha]:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9993558830083456`*^9, 3.9993559084544706`*^9}, 
   3.999355958826875*^9, {3.999355997869217*^9, 
   3.9993559986294584`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d2172c5e-56ea-4eea-a331-\
6b2ffc536506"],

Cell[BoxData[
 StyleBox[
  RowBox[{
   SubscriptBox["T", "GL"], "(", "t", ")", "=", 
   SuperscriptBox[
    RowBox[{"(", 
     RowBox[{"1", "+", 
      FractionBox[
       RowBox[{"\[Sigma]", " ", "t"}], "\[Beta]"]}], ")"}], 
    RowBox[{"-", "\[Alpha]"}]]}],
  FontSize->24,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0.2, 0.4, 0.8]]], "DisplayFormula",ExpressionUUID->\
"ae851810-ce91-488e-81c5-6ff617c2d2cb"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Here, by contrast, \[Sigma] itself is a ",
 StyleBox["randomized",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " extinction rate, drawn from a Gamma distribution with shape \[Alpha] and \
scale \[Beta] -- the transmittance above ",
 StyleBox["is",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " the ensemble average \[LeftAngleBracket]",
 StyleBox["Exp",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "[-\[Sigma] t]\[RightAngleBracket] over that distribution, not a curve \
chosen to resemble one. \[Alpha] controls how variable the rate is across \
realizations (smaller \[Alpha] means a heavier, more clustered medium); \
\[Beta] sets its scale. Because Exp[-\[Sigma] t] is convex in \[Sigma], \
Jensen\[CloseCurlyQuote]s inequality applies unconditionally here: this \
transmittance can only ever sit at or above classical, for any choice of \
\[Alpha] and \[Beta] whatsoever."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ee9173ab-57f8-4b83-ab39-000e0a2ac770"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[Alpha]=1 is not an arbitrary point to check -- it is the specific case \
where the Gamma distribution over \[Sigma] collapses to a ",
 StyleBox["plain exponential distribution",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " (Gamma with shape 1 is exponential, exactly, not approximately). ",
 StyleBox["That",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " is the real reason the identity below lands where it does: \n- one formula \
comes from a fixed rate with a reweighted event count, \n- the other from an \
exponentially-distributed randomized rate \ntwo genuinely different \
mechanisms, meeting at the one point where the second one\[CloseCurlyQuote]s \
own randomness takes its simplest possible form."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.999326061705427*^9, 
  3.9993260828766003`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2eb760b5-a701-449a-a6dd-\
58ae94aea385"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["[see -> earlier derivation, following Davis & Xu (DX14), cited by \
Wrenninge et al. 2017]\n",
  FontSize->12,
  FontColor->GrayLevel[0.5]],
 "\[Alpha] has a precise statistical meaning, not just a qualitative one. If \
\[Sigma] is Gamma-distributed with mean ",
 StyleBox["\[Sigma]bar",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " and shape \[Alpha], its coefficient of variation is fixed entirely by \
\[Alpha]:"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b62e8504-02cc-46eb-8560-daf68a54fd0e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

