Cell["\<\
Now both panels use the identical i-to-distance mapping -- the only remaining \
difference between them is that the left panel is a genuine Monte Carlo \
estimate (noisy, finite nRndSamples) and the right is the exact closed form \
(smooth, zero noise).\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9993567074182577`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"60ca8075-67c1-49a0-ab72-\
43396e739214"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Classical as the Zero-Variance Member of the Same Family", "Subchapter",ExpressionUUID->"2bf5d150-8749-4e2a-8670-c19fc987a52c"],

Cell["\<\
A single sigma, as used everywhere in classical transport, can itself be \
understood inside this framework -- not as a categorically different kind of \
object, but as the mean of a degenerate distribution: one with exactly zero \
variance, every realization giving the identical value. The \
\[OpenCurlyQuote]average\[CloseCurlyQuote] of a distribution with no spread \
at all is trivially just that value itself.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2cdbcabd-b73f-492b-af3f-ffbb8a4884a3"],

Cell["\<\
This is not a loose reinterpretation -- it is exactly the limit already \
established rigorously. Var->0 is precisely the equality condition in Jensen\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s inequality, the one case where the ensemble average \
collapses back to plain classical exactly:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"2a275daf-cbbb-4c57-a0e7-da39d7dee8c3"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Limit[TGL[s, sigmabar, alpha, alpha], alpha -> Infinity]", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[57]:=",ExpressionUUID->"62aaee74-591f-4ed5-b356-d3867dc60996"],

Cell[BoxData[
 SuperscriptBox["\[ExponentialE]", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"-", "s"}], " ", "sigmabar"}]]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.9992752845252504`*^9, 3.999275667327318*^9, 
  3.999321812121901*^9, 3.9993225378165956`*^9, 3.999324478245257*^9, 
  3.9993470010452747`*^9, 3.9993470375381956`*^9, 3.9993481252245035`*^9, 
  3.999350516305388*^9, 3.9993508945420923`*^9, 3.9993512078858137`*^9, 
  3.9993512483328943`*^9, 3.9993556936535935`*^9, 3.9993570999677076`*^9, 
  3.99935783086172*^9, 3.9994094967765203`*^9, 3.99940988933843*^9, 
  3.9994107757629523`*^9, 3.999411697790777*^9, 3.9994122944628286`*^9, 
  3.9994123729940004`*^9, 3.999413118916786*^9, 3.999413248917967*^9, 
  3.9995011471827664`*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[57]=",ExpressionUUID->"96ff5485-5bb4-4f25-b57d-c020d9ff51c2"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
(* Expect: Exp[-s*sigmabar] exactly -- confirmed symbolically, not just as a \
numerical trend *)\
\>", "Input",
 CellLabel->"In[58]:=",ExpressionUUID->"9a25cfa8-b441-4751-ba98-9c397df0deb4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Take care",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " which end of the family this actually is. CV",
 Cell[BoxData[
  SuperscriptBox["", "2"]],ExpressionUUID->
  "8aac0cb0-04dd-4003-888f-f367d4888076"],
 "=1/\[Alpha] means zero variance sits at \[Alpha]\[Rule]\[Infinity], not \
\[Alpha]=0 -- \[Alpha]\[Rule]0 is the opposite extreme, infinite variance, \
the most extreme clumping the family can reach, already visible in the CV(\
\[Alpha]) plot above where the curve diverges as \[Alpha]\[Rule]0 rather than \
vanishing."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a04b51fc-8842-4d60-a418-e599a18544a6"],

Cell["\<\
So the single number \[Sigma]bar plugged into any member of this family -- \
classical, Gamma-Lomax at any \[Alpha], SumOfExp, Erlang -- is always, \
uniformly, \[OpenCurlyQuote]the mean of whatever distribution governs the \
real material.\[CloseCurlyQuote] What changes from one member to the next is \
not whether averaging happened -- it always did -- but how much spread that \
underlying distribution has. Classical is not a categorically different case \
under this view. It is simply the \[Alpha]\[Rule]\[Infinity] member of the \
same family already used throughout this notebook.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"e850faeb-2551-41b4-b697-c041f8daa31a"],

Cell["\<\
Two specific, meaningful points on the same CV(\[Alpha]) axis, now both \
identified precisely:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"cf04ed64-7272-4614-bb99-1468fe7489e8"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Plot[1/Sqrt[a], {a, 0.05, 8}, 
Epilog -> {RGBColor[0.2, 0.4, 0.8], 
PointSize[0.02], Point[{1, 1}], 
GrayLevel[0.5], 
Text[\"alpha=1, CV=1\\nhyperbolic identity\", {1.9, 1.25}], 
{RGBColor[0.8, 0.0, 0.0], Dashed, Line[{{6, 0}, {6, 0.41}}]}, 
Text[\"alpha->infinity\\nCV->0, classical\", {6, 0.55}]}, 
AxesLabel -> {\"alpha\", \"CV\"}, 
PlotLabel -> \"The same axis carries both findings of this notebook\", \
PlotStyle -> RGBColor[0.2, 0.4, 0.8], ImageSize -> Full]\
\>", "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.99927569994871*^9, 3.9992757252141047`*^9}, 
